Title: THE LAST HOUR
Credit: Written by
Author: The ClearFrame team
Draft date: August 2026
Contact: clearframe.dev

====

INT. RECORD SHOP — DAY

Scene 1.

Rain against glass. NOOR (30s) files vinyl into a crate. Behind the counter, a
hand-painted mural of a whale runs the length of the wall, signed in the corner.

A speaker crackles. A slow, modern COVER of "AFTER THE BALL" plays — a woman's
voice, close-mic'd, nothing like the wax-cylinder original.

NOOR
My grandmother had this on a cylinder.
The real one. This one's a fake.

DEV (20s) leans on the counter, drinking from a can of COCA-COLA, the logo facing
camera.

DEV
Everything's a cover of something.

INT. RECORD SHOP — BACK ROOM — DAY

Scene 2.

A cramped office. On the wall, a framed print of Hokusai's THE GREAT WAVE OFF
KANAGAWA, sun-bleached. Beneath it, a laptop with a half-peeled STARBUCKS sticker.

Noor opens a laptop. On screen: black-and-white ARCHIVAL NEWSREEL FOOTAGE of a
1936 harbour strike, timecode burned into the corner.

NOOR (V.O.)
The shop's been here since the strike.
My great-grandfather is in this footage.
Third from the left, if you believe him.

EXT. HARBOUR — DAY

Scene 3.

Cranes. Gulls. A container ship with the MAERSK livery crosses frame, slow,
unmissable.

Dev photographs it on a phone.

DEV
Ninety years and it's still the same water.

INT. NOOR'S FLAT — NIGHT

Scene 4.

A small kitchen. Noor cooks. On the radio, a news presenter's voice.

RADIO PRESENTER (V.O.)
...and in a statement today, the Prime
Minister said the port deal would go ahead.

Noor turns the radio off. On the fridge, a postcard of a FRIDA KAHLO self-portrait,
curling at the corners.

She reads aloud from a paperback, the cover clearly visible: ONE HUNDRED YEARS OF
SOLITUDE.

NOOR
"Many years later, as he faced the firing
squad..."

INT. RECORD SHOP — NIGHT

Scene 5.

Noor alone, closing up. She plays the cover of AFTER THE BALL again, louder.

She lifts a crate. Underneath: a poster for a 1977 concert, the venue long gone,
the band's logo unmistakable.

NOOR
(to nobody)
Everything's a cover of something.

FADE OUT.
